From: djsaunde <1245942+djsaunde@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 10:18:56 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 08/21] fast: depth read-back rows: flip them to the
 framebuffer's row order

GetPixelDepth takes top-down game coordinates. Fast3D draws in two row
orders, and the lookup must match the target it reads:
- straight into the window (mRendersToFb false: a port without the LUS
  GUI), or with MSAA into a window-sized framebuffer drawn the same way
  up: the rows run bottom-up, and the lookup read the vertically
  mirrored pixel. y is flipped there.
- into the game framebuffer at a render size other than the view's (an
  internal resolution; macOS always): the framebuffer is drawn flipped
  (invertY), so its rows run top-down, but GetPixelDepth flips an
  invertY framebuffer's rows (height - y), so it read the mirrored row
  too. y is pre-flipped there, cancelling GetPixelDepth's flip.

Found by the 1080 port's golden tests (patch 3's depth read-back on the
split-screen scene read the bottom window's depth for the top window),
and against real hardware: with an internal resolution or MSAA the sun
visibility test (9 depth reads) answered for the mirrored pixels, so the
sun and lens flare of 1080's Round 1 intro were drawn differently.
---
 src/fast/interpreter.cpp | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/fast/interpreter.cpp b/src/fast/interpreter.cpp
index a410c20..57ddc3b 100644
--- a/src/fast/interpreter.cpp
+++ b/src/fast/interpreter.cpp
@@ -5475,6 +5475,16 @@ void Interpreter::AdjustPixelDepthCoordinates(float& x, float& y) {
     x = x * RATIO_X(mActiveFrameBuffer, mCurDimensions) -
         (mNativeDimensions.width * RATIO_X(mActiveFrameBuffer, mCurDimensions) - mCurDimensions.width) / 2;
     y *= RATIO_Y(mActiveFrameBuffer, mCurDimensions);
+    if (!mRendersToFb || ViewportMatchesRendererResolution()) {
+        // the game drew into the window, or (MSAA) into a window-sized framebuffer drawn the same way up: rows run
+        // bottom-up
+        y = mGameWindowViewport.height - 1 - y;
+    } else {
+        // the game framebuffer (a render size other than the view's; macOS / OpenGL ES always) is drawn flipped
+        // (invertY), so its rows run top-down, and GetPixelDepth flips an invertY framebuffer's rows (height - y):
+        // cancel that, so the pixel read is the one the window path reads
+        y = mCurDimensions.height - y;
+    }
     if (!mRendersToFb || (mMsaaLevel > 1 && mCurDimensions.width == mGameWindowViewport.width &&
                           mCurDimensions.height == mGameWindowViewport.height)) {
         x += mGameWindowViewport.x;
